\chapter{The PEG Definition Grammar: Rules and Channels}
\label{ch:peg-definition}

\section{What a PEG Definition Is}

A parsing expression grammar, in the classical sense, is a grammar in which
every production is written as an expression: a sequence, an ordered choice, a
repetition, a lookahead, or a reference to another production. The defining
property of the family is that ordered choice \emph{never} backtracks once it
has committed, which removes the exponential blow-up that a context-free
grammar suffers from on ambiguous input. It is a pleasant thing to program
against, and it is the right tool for the large majority of languages that are
not deliberately ambiguous.

\DSLtk{} ships a PEG subsystem, and it is worth being precise about what that
subsystem is, because it is not what a reader who has seen the literature
would expect. The subsystem occupies lines 8123 to 8884 of
\code{DSLtk.hpp} and divides into two layers. The first layer, which this
chapter covers, is the \emph{definition} layer: a flat, ordered set of
patterns, each attached to a channel and optionally to a semantic action,
together with a driver that walks the input left to right and fires the first
pattern that matches at each position. The second layer, covered in
\chapref{ch:peg-combinators}, wraps the ordinary \code{dsl::Parser}
machinery of \chapref{ch:parser-core} in strict PEG semantics and gives you
sequence, ordered choice, repetition and lookahead as first-class
combinators.

The single most important fact about the definition layer is this: a rule is
\emph{not} a production. It is not a string of the form
\code{"name <- expr"}, and it is not a callable that is invoked on the
remaining input. It is a \emph{compile-time pattern string} written in a
small regular-expression subset, and the pattern is matched against the
input at the current position as a prefix. The rule set, taken together, is
therefore a \emph{lexer}, not a grammar: it decides what the next token is,
and it does so by trying each rule in declaration order and taking the first
one that matches. Structure above the token level -- nesting, recursion,
precedence -- is not expressible here, and Chapter~\ref{ch:peg-combinators}
and Chapter~\ref{ch:peg-semantic-values} exist precisely to explain what to
do about that.

This design has one large advantage and one large cost, and both are worth
stating up front. The advantage is that the whole definition layer is a
single linear scan over the rules at each position, with no backtracking and
no recursion, so it is linear in the length of the input times the number of
rules, and it never risks a stack overflow. The cost is that the expressive
power of a rule is exactly the expressive power of a regular expression
subset, and a user who reaches for \code{PEGDefinition} expecting to write a
grammar will find themselves writing a tokenizer instead. The examples that
ship with the toolkit, in the \code{examples} directory of the source tree,
are a good illustration: \code{23-peg-definition.cpp} declares a rule per
token kind, and the semantic actions print the tokens.

\section{Channels}

A channel is a named logical stream that matches are routed into. The type is
small enough to describe in full:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The channel type and the two built-in channels, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8147--8165.},label={lst:ch25-channel}]
struct PEGChannel
{
  std::string name{ "@DEFAULT" };

  constexpr bool operator== (const PEGChannel &) const = default;

  bool
  is_ignore () const noexcept
  {
    return name == "@IGNORE";
  }
};

inline const PEGChannel PEGIgnoreChannel{ "@IGNORE" };
inline const PEGChannel PEGDefaultChannel{ "@DEFAULT" };
\end{lstlisting}

A default-constructed \code{PEGChannel} is named \code{"@DEFAULT"}, so a rule
that is never configured belongs to the default channel. The equality operator
is defaulted, which means two channels are equal exactly when their names are
equal; nothing else distinguishes them. In particular there is no channel
identifier, no channel registry, and no way to ask a definition which
channels it uses -- \code{PEGDefinition} has no \code{channels()} method.

The only channel with behaviour attached to it is \code{"@IGNORE"}. The
predicate \code{is_ignore()} tests for that name by string comparison, and
the streaming matcher of \chapref{ch:peg-combinators} uses it to decide
which rules to swallow silently. Every other channel is a pure label: it is
recorded in the match, and it is what the matcher's \code{only},
\code{include} and \code{exclude} filters compare against.

Custom channels are created with \code{new_peg_channel}, and the function
validates its argument:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8176--8183. \code{new_peg_channel} refuses any name that does not begin with \code{@}.},label={lst:ch25-newchannel}]
inline PEGChannel
new_peg_channel (std::string_view name)
{
  if (name.empty () || name.front () != '@')
    throw std::invalid_argument (
        "dsl::new_peg_channel: channel names must begin with '@'");
  return PEGChannel{ std::string (name) };
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{@} prefix is a namespace convention for the channel name space. It
keeps user channels from colliding with the two built-in names, and it makes
the reserved space obvious in a grammar that is read by other people. The
price is that the function throws, and because the check lives in a free
function rather than in a constructor, the exception escapes from the call
that creates the channel rather than from some later use of it. A grammar
that computes channel names at run time must therefore either validate
them itself or accept that a bad name aborts the parse setup with
\code{std::invalid_argument}. Because \code{new_peg_channel} takes a
\code{std::string_view} and copies it into a \code{std::string}, the returned
channel owns its name and stays valid for as long as it is held.

\section{The Pattern Language}

The pattern language is implemented by \code{peg_detail::CompiledPattern}, a
small struct with a vector of atoms and two status fields
(\code{DSLtk.hpp}, lines 8218 to 8368). Each atom records whether it is the
wildcard, a character class or a literal, the class body, and a quantifier
character. The compiler, \code{CompiledPattern::compile}, is a flat scanner:
it walks the pattern once, emits one atom per literal, per wildcard and per
character class, and attaches a quantifier to an atom when one immediately
follows it. There is no recursion in the compiler and no tree of any kind in
the result, which is why the pattern language is so small.

Table~\ref{tab:ch25-pattern} is the complete syntax.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{The pattern language accepted by \code{CompiledPattern}. The
compiler is a single left-to-right scan; there is no notion of a group, an
alternative, or a nested quantifier.}
\label{tab:ch25-pattern}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.16\textwidth}L{0.29\textwidth}L{0.40\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Construct} & \textbf{Matches} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
\code{c} & the literal character \code{c} & Any character that is not a
metacharacter is a literal, including \code{|}, \code{(}, \code{)} and
and a backslash. \\
\code{.} & any one character & Matches a newline as readily as a letter.
There is no "any but newline" option. \\
\code{[abc]} & one character from the set & The class ends at the first
\code{]}. A \code{]} elsewhere in the class is not expressible. \\
\code{[a-z0-9]} & one character from a range & A range is recognised when
the middle of three consecutive characters is \code{-}. A trailing \code{-}
is a literal. \\
\code{[^abc]} & one character \emph{not} in the set & Only a
\code{^} in the first position negates; elsewhere it is an ordinary
class member. \\
\code{*} & zero or more of the preceding atom & Greedy. It never gives back
characters it has already consumed. \\
\code{+} & one or more of the preceding atom & Greedy, and it fails outright
if it matches nothing. \\
\code{?} & zero or one of the preceding atom & Greedy: it takes the
character when one is available. \\
\code{!} & the preceding atom must \emph{not} match here & Zero-width
negative lookahead. The atom is not consumed. \\
\code{^} & nothing & Accepted and ignored. \\
\code{$} & nothing & Accepted and ignored. \\
\midrule
\code{|} & the literal character \code{|} & There is no alternation in a
rule pattern. Alternation is expressed by the rule set, which is tried in
order. \\
\code{(} \code{)} & the literal characters & There is no grouping. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two of those rows deserve emphasis, because both are places where the
shipped code differs from what the notation suggests.

The first is the treatment of \code{^} and \code{$}. They are accepted
and silently discarded by the compiler. The reason is that a rule is always
matched at the current position of the input and never searches forward, so
the anchors carry no information; a pattern that begins with \code{^}
behaves exactly like the same pattern without it. This is worth knowing
because it means a pattern copied from a conventional regular expression will
behave differently from the way it looks: \code{"^[a-z]+$"} is
not "a whole word", it is "a run of letters", and it will happily match the
first three letters of \code{"abc"}.

The second is the absence of escape sequences. The compiler has no notion of a
tab escape, a digit escape, a word escape or a newline escape: a backslash is
an ordinary class member or an ordinary literal. The consequence is that the
C++ spelling of a pattern and the pattern itself are not the same string. The
pattern that matches whitespace has to be written in the C++ source as
\texttt{"[ \textbackslash t\textbackslash n]+"} -- a literal in which
\texttt{\textbackslash t} denotes a real tab character and
\texttt{\textbackslash n} a real newline -- because that is what has to reach
the compiler. Writing the doubled spelling
\texttt{"[ \textbackslash\textbackslash t\textbackslash\textbackslash n]+"} in the
source, by contrast, produces the eight-character pattern
\texttt{[ \textbackslash\textbackslash t\textbackslash\textbackslash n]+}, whose class
body is the five characters space, backslash, \code{t}, backslash, \code{n};
that class matches a space, a backslash, the letter \code{t} or the letter
\code{n}, and nothing else.

This is not a hypothetical. The shipped example
\code{23-peg-definition.cpp} writes its whitespace rule with the
doubled spelling, which is the broken one. Run against its own sample input,
that example
prints \code{skip_ws:2} where a single space was expected, and then
\code{id:hen} where the word \code{then} was expected: the rule consumed the
space and the \code{t} of \code{then}, and the identifier rule matched what
was left. The same example's derived grammar prints \code{num:0} followed by
\code{id:x10} for the input \code{0x10}, because the parent's number rule
matches the leading \code{0} before the derived hexadecimal rule is ever
consulted. Both effects are the subject of this chapter; they are properties
of the shipped code, not of a hypothetical.

The third row of Table~\ref{tab:ch25-pattern} that deserves comment is the
one about greediness. The matching function \code{match_at} of
\code{CompiledPattern} is a greedy prefix match: it walks the atoms in
order, and for a \code{*} or \code{+} atom it consumes as many characters
as it can and never retreats. So the pattern
\code{"a+ab"} does not match \code{"aab"}, because the \code{a+} swallows
both \code{a}s and the trailing \code{ab} then fails. The \code{*} branch of
\code{dsl::pattern<>} backtracks and this one does not; the two engines are
similar but not identical, and the difference is observable.

The compiler reports one kind of error. If a \code{[} is not closed by a
\code{]}, the result has \code{valid} set to false and \code{error} set to
\code{"unterminated character class"}. The pattern is not otherwise
interpreted.

\section{Rules}

A rule is a compiled pattern plus a set of mutable behaviours.
\code{PEGRule} is a plain aggregate with no invariants and no private state,
and every member can be changed after the rule has been created.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{The members of \code{PEGRule} (\code{DSLtk.hpp}, lines 8410--8447).}
\label{tab:ch25-rule}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.21\textwidth}L{0.19\textwidth}L{0.45\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Member} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
\code{pattern_text} & \code{std::string} & The pattern exactly as it was
supplied, kept for diagnostics and for the \code{pattern_text} label that
\code{peg_rule} reports on failure. \\
\code{compiled} & \code{CompiledPattern} & The compiled form. Public, so a
caller may inspect the atoms, but there is no supported way to recompile it
in place. \\
\code{semantic_action} & \code{PEGSemanticAction} & A
\code{std::function<void(PEGMatch&)>}, invoked when the rule matches. See
\chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values}. \\
\code{channel} & \code{PEGChannel} & The channel the match is routed into.
Defaults to \code{"@DEFAULT"}. \\
\code{must_fail} & \code{bool} & Turns the rule into a negative rule: it
succeeds only where the pattern does \emph{not} match. \\
\code{fire_on_neg} & \code{bool} & When the rule is negated, still run its
semantic action on the negated success. \\
\code{if_succeed} & \code{PEGRule *} & A second rule to attempt at the
same position once this one has matched. \\
\code{order} & \code{std::size_t} & The declaration order, assigned by the
definition that created the rule. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two of these members are unusual enough to deserve a paragraph each.

\code{must_fail} implements the negative rule. The method that does the
work is \code{try_match}, and its behaviour for a negated rule is not the
obvious one:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8424--8435. \code{PEGRule::try_match}. A negated rule succeeds by consuming exactly one character, so that a tokenizing parse always makes progress.},label={lst:ch25-trymatch}]
std::optional<std::size_t>
try_match (std::string_view text, std::size_t pos) const
{
  if (must_fail)
    {
      if (compiled.match_at (text, pos))
        return std::nullopt;
      return pos < text.size () ? std::optional<std::size_t> (pos + 1)
                                : std::nullopt;
    }
  return compiled.match_at (text, pos);
}
\end{lstlisting}

Read it carefully. For a negated rule, \code{try_match} returns
\code{std::nullopt} when the pattern \emph{does} match -- that is, the
negated rule fails exactly where its pattern succeeds. When the pattern does
not match, the negated rule succeeds, and it succeeds by consuming exactly
one character. That is a design decision rather than a natural consequence:
a rule that consumed nothing would leave the driver in a loop, so the negated
rule eats one character to guarantee progress. The practical consequence is
that a negated rule is a \emph{one-character scanner}, not a zero-width
assertion. If you want to assert that the next character is not a digit, a
negated rule will consume that character for you, and you must be prepared
for it.

The second unusual member is \code{if_succeed}. It is a pointer to another
rule that is attempted at the \emph{same} position as the rule that owns it,
immediately after that rule has matched and its action has run. If the
second rule also matches, its action runs as well. The pointer is followed
exactly one level deep: the second rule's own \code{if_succeed} is ignored.

The rule also has one piece of sugar. The call operator is overloaded, so a
rule can be turned into a match arm by calling it with a callback:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8438--8446. The call operator on \code{PEGRule} builds a matcher arm that binds the rule to a callback.},label={lst:ch25-arm}]
template <typename Fn>
peg_detail::PEGArm
operator() (Fn &&fn) const
{
  peg_detail::PEGArm arm;
  arm.rule = const_cast<PEGRule *> (this);
  arm.action = std::forward<Fn> (fn);
  return arm;
}
\end{lstlisting}

This is what makes the streaming matcher of \chapref{ch:peg-combinators}
readable: pushing a rule with a callback is the natural spelling of "try
this rule next".

\section{Grammars}

\code{PEGDefinition} is the container. It holds a pointer to an optional
parent definition, a vector of local rules, and a counter used to assign
declaration order. Two free functions create definitions:
\code{create_peg_definition()} returns a root definition, and
\code{derive_peg_definition(parent)} returns a child whose parent rules
remain visible.

The interesting method is \code{rules()}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8528--8537. \code{PEGDefinition::rules()} flattens the inheritance chain into one ordered list.},label={lst:ch25-rules}]
std::vector<const PEGRule *>
rules () const
{
  std::vector<const PEGRule *> out;
  if (parent)
    out = parent->rules ();
  for (const auto &r : local_rules_)
    out.push_back (&r);
  return out;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The flattening is parent first, then local. That single sentence explains
most of the surprising behaviour of derived grammars. Because the parent
rules come first in the ordered choice, a derived rule can only ever be
reached where none of the parent's rules match. A derived rule that
duplicates a parent rule's pattern is therefore dead code, and the
shadowing that the class documentation promises -- "may add new rules or
shadow parent behavior without mutating the parent" -- does not happen
through the ordered choice at all. It can only happen if the parent's rule
fails where the child's would succeed, which is a much weaker claim.

There is a second, smaller inheritance problem. The declaration order counter
\code{next_order_} is a member of the definition, so a derived definition
starts counting again from zero. The \code{order} field of a rule is
therefore only comparable among rules that came from the same definition. It
is a useful debugging aid, not a global precedence key.

Rules are added with \code{add_rule}, which comes in two flavours:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8511--8524. The two forms of \code{add_rule}. The pattern is a compile-time \code{FixedString}, so it must be a string literal.},label={lst:ch25-addrule}]
template <FixedString S, typename Fn>
PEGRule &
add_rule (Fn &&action)
{
  return emplace_rule (S.view (),
                       PEGSemanticAction (std::forward<Fn> (action)));
}

template <FixedString S>
PEGRule &
add_rule ()
{
  return emplace_rule (S.view (), PEGSemanticAction{});
}
\end{lstlisting}

The pattern is a template argument, which means it must be a string literal
known at compile time; there is no way to add a rule whose pattern is
computed at run time. The return value is a reference to the stored rule,
and \code{emplace_rule} assigns its \code{order} as it goes:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8620--8634. \code{emplace_rule} compiles the pattern, rejects an invalid one, and assigns the declaration order.},label={lst:ch25-emplace}]
PEGRule &
emplace_rule (std::string_view pat, PEGSemanticAction action)
{
  PEGRule r;
  r.pattern_text = std::string (pat);
  r.compiled = peg_detail::CompiledPattern::compile (pat);
  if (!r.compiled.valid)
    throw std::invalid_argument ("dsl::PEGDefinition::add_rule: "
                                 + r.compiled.error + " in pattern '"
                                 + r.pattern_text + "'");
  r.semantic_action = std::move (action);
  r.order = next_order_++;
  local_rules_.push_back (std::move (r));
  return local_rules_.back ();
}
\end{lstlisting}

Note that the compile happens eagerly, at the moment the rule is added, and
that an invalid pattern throws \code{std::invalid_argument} immediately
rather than at parse time. That is a good property: a grammar with a broken
pattern fails at construction.

Note also what \code{emplace_rule} returns: a reference into the vector of
rules that the definition holds. Adding another rule may reallocate that
vector, and a reallocation invalidates every reference and pointer into
it. The comment above \code{add_rule}
claims that the reference "stays valid as long as rules are only appended",
and that claim is false. Section~\ref{sec:ch25-limits} returns to this
point, because it is the sharpest edge in the whole subsystem.

\section{Diagnostics}

\code{PEGParseResult} is the outcome of a parse. It holds a success flag, the
offset at which the parse stopped, the line and column of that offset, and a
message.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{The accessors of \code{PEGParseResult} (\code{DSLtk.hpp}, lines
8452--8491). All of them are read-only views over the same four fields.}
\label{tab:ch25-result}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}L{0.27\textwidth}L{0.21\textwidth}L{0.37\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Accessor} & \textbf{Returns} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
\code{ok()} & \code{bool} & True when the whole input was consumed. \\
\code{failed()} & \code{bool} & The negation of \code{ok()}. \\
\code{error_offset()} & \code{std::size_t} & The byte offset at which the
parse stopped. \\
\code{error_line()} & \code{std::size_t} & The one-based line of that
offset. \\
\code{error_column()} & \code{std::size_t} & The one-based column of that
offset. \\
\code{error_message()} & \code{std::string_view} & A human-readable
description; empty on success. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The line and column are computed by \code{peg_detail::line_col_at}, a
function that walks the text from the beginning counting newlines. It is
called once per match and once per failure, and it is therefore quadratic in
the length of the input for a long token stream. For a source of a few
thousand characters this is irrelevant; for a large file it is the first
thing you would want to replace.

The failure messages the driver produces are fixed strings. There are exactly
two of them, and they are worth quoting in full because they are the only
diagnostics a user gets: \code{"PEG rule matched empty input; refusing to
loop"} and \code{"no PEG rule matched at offset N"}. There is no list of
what was expected at the failure point, which is the one diagnostic feature
that the parser core of \chapref{ch:parser-core} has and the PEG definition
layer lacks.

\section{The Ordered-Choice Loop}

The driver itself is transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 8546 to 8617. The
following pseudocode is that code with the control flow flattened.

\begin{pseudocode}[Tokenizing with ordered choice]
\textbf{function} tokenize (definition, text)\\
\quad rules = definition.rules() \quad // parent rules first, then local\\
\quad pos = 0\\
\quad \textbf{while} pos < length(text) \textbf{do}\\
\qquad matched = false\\
\qquad \textbf{for each} rule r \textbf{in} rules \textbf{in} order \textbf{do}\\
\qquad\quad end = r.try\_match(text, pos)\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{if} end is empty \textbf{then} \textbf{continue}\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{if} r is positive \textbf{and} end = pos \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{fail} ("PEG rule matched empty input; refusing to loop")\\
\qquad\quad matched = true\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{if} r is positive \textbf{or} r.fire\_on\_neg \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad m = PEGMatch(text[pos .. end), pos, end, r, r.channel)\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} r has an action \textbf{then} r.action(m)\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{if} r.if\_succeed is not empty \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad e = r.if\_succeed.try\_match(text, pos)\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} e is not empty \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad\quad run the action of r.if\_succeed at the same position\\
\qquad\quad pos = end\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{break} \quad // ordered choice: the first success wins\\
\qquad \textbf{if} not matched \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\quad \textbf{fail} ("no PEG rule matched at offset " + pos)\\
\quad \textbf{return} success at pos
\end{pseudocode}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch25-loop} draws the same control flow.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dslnode] (start) {\texttt{pos = 0}};
  \node[dsldecision, below=11mm of start] (more) {input left?};
  \node[dslterm, left=32mm of more] (done) {success};

  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of more] (loop) {for each rule \texttt{r}\\\texttt{in} declaration order};
  \node[dsldecision, below=13mm of loop] (try) {\texttt{r.try\_match}\\\texttt{succeeded?}};
  \node[dslterm, right=34mm of try] (fail) {failure:\\"no PEG rule matched\\at offset N"};

  \node[dsldecision, below=13mm of try] (zero) {positive rule\\\texttt{and} zero width?};
  \node[dslterm, left=32mm of zero] (looperr) {failure:\\"matched empty input;\\refusing to loop"};

  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of zero] (fire) {build the \texttt{PEGMatch};\\fire the semantic action};
  \node[dslbox, below=11mm of fire] (adv) {\texttt{pos = end};\quad \texttt{break}};

  \draw[dsledge] (start) -- (more);
  \draw[dsledge] (more) -- node[right, font=\scriptsize] {yes} (loop);
  \draw[dsledge] (more) -- node[above, font=\scriptsize] {no} (done);
  \draw[dsledge] (loop) -- (try);
  \draw[dsledge] (try) -- node[right, font=\scriptsize] {yes} (zero);
  \draw[dsledge] (zero) -- node[right, font=\scriptsize] {no} (fire);
  \draw[dsledge] (fire) -- (adv);

  \draw[dslfeedback] (try.west) -- ++(-13mm,0) |- node[left, font=\scriptsize] {no: the next rule} (loop.west);
  \draw[dsledgedash] (try.east) -- node[above, font=\scriptsize] {exhausted} (fail.west);
  \draw[dsledge] (zero.west) -- node[below, font=\scriptsize] {yes} (looperr.east);
  \draw[dslfeedback] (adv.east) -- ++(27mm,0) |- node[below, font=\scriptsize] {the next token} (more.east);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{The ordered-choice loop of \code{PEGDefinition::parse}. The rules
are tried in declaration order at each position; the first success wins and
the cursor advances to the end of the match. There is no backtracking across
