another.}
\label{fig:ch25-loop}
\end{figure}

Four properties of this loop are worth stating explicitly, because they are
the difference between a PEG and a backtracking regular expression matcher.

First, the choice is \emph{local}. Each rule is tried at the current
position only. If rule three matches at position ten and rule one would have
matched at position ten had it been tried first, rule one never gets its
chance, because rule three came first in the list.

Second, the choice is \emph{ordered by declaration}. The order of the
\code{add_rule} calls is the precedence order of the grammar. This is why
the shipped example puts its keyword rules before its identifier rule, and
why putting \code{"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"} first would shadow every
keyword.

Third, there is no backtracking across positions. If the chosen rule turns
out to be wrong in a wider context, the driver does not go back and try
another rule; the rule set has already committed.

Fourth, a zero-width positive match is treated as a fatal error rather than
an infinite loop. The check is made before the action fires, so a rule whose
pattern can match the empty string -- \code{"a*"}, \code{"[a-z]?"}, or the
empty pattern itself -- aborts the parse with the message "PEG rule matched
empty input; refusing to loop" rather than hanging. This is a guard against
a mistake, and it is a good one, but note that it aborts the whole parse
rather than skipping the rule.

\section{A Worked Example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-grammar} is a complete program. It builds a small
tokenizing grammar for a toy assignment language, prints the rules in
ordered-choice order, parses a sample sentence, and then shows what happens
when the input contains a character that no rule accepts.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A tokenizing PEG grammar with two channels. The \code{@IGNORE} rule swallows whitespace; the \code{@KEYWORD} rule tags keywords.},label={lst:ch25-grammar}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace
{
struct Token
{
  std::string kind;
  std::string text;
  std::size_t line{ 1 };
  std::size_t column{ 1 };
  bool ignored{ false };
};

void
record (std::vector<Token> &sink, char const *kind, dsl::PEGMatch &m)
{
  sink.push_back (Token{ kind, std::string (m.value), m.line, m.column,
                         m.ignored });
}

/// The pattern compiler understands no escape sequences, so a pattern
/// that contains a real tab prints with a real tab.  Show it escaped.
std::string
show (std::string_view s)
{
  std::string out;
  for (char c : s)
    {
      if (c == '\t')
        out += "\\t";
      else if (c == '\n')
        out += "\\n";
      else
        out += c;
    }
  return out;
}
} // namespace

int
main ()
{
  std::vector<Token> tokens;

  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  // The reference returned by add_rule is valid only until the next
  // add_rule call, so every rule is configured in its own scope.
  {
    auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \t\n]+"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "space", m); });
    ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
  }
  {
    auto &kw = grammar.add_rule<"let"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "keyword", m); });
    kw.channel = dsl::new_peg_channel ("@KEYWORD");
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "ident", m); });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "number", m); });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"+"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "op", m); });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"="> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "op", m); });
  }

  std::cout << "visible rules in ordered-choice order:\n";
  for (const dsl::PEGRule *r : grammar.rules ())
    std::cout << "  #" << r->order << "  \"" << show (r->pattern_text)
              << "\"  channel=" << r->channel.name << "\n";

  auto result = grammar.parse ("let x = 12 + y");

  std::cout << "\ntokens of \"let x = 12 + y\":\n";
  for (const Token &t : tokens)
    if (!t.ignored)
      std::cout << "  " << t.kind << " \"" << t.text << "\"  at " << t.line
                << ":" << t.column << "\n";

  std::cout << "\nparse " << (result.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed")
            << ", offset " << result.offset << "\n";

  auto bad = grammar.parse ("let x = ;");
  std::cout << "parse of \"let x = ;\" " << (bad.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed")
            << " at line " << bad.error_line () << " column "
            << bad.error_column () << ": " << bad.error_message () << "\n";

  auto derived = dsl::derive_peg_definition (grammar);
  {
    auto &hex = derived.add_rule<"0x[0-9a-fA-F]+"> (
        [&tokens] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { record (tokens, "hex", m); });
    (void) hex;
  }
  std::cout << "\nderived grammar sees " << derived.rules ().size ()
            << " rules (6 inherited, 1 local)\n";

  tokens.clear ();
  auto dr = derived.parse ("0x1f");
  std::cout << "derived parse of \"0x1f\" "
            << (dr.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed") << ":\n";
  for (const Token &t : tokens)
    if (!t.ignored)
      std::cout << "  " << t.kind << " \"" << t.text << "\"\n";

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is shown in Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-grammar-out}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-grammar}.},label={lst:ch25-grammar-out}]
visible rules in ordered-choice order:
  #0  "[ \t\n]+"  channel=@IGNORE
  #1  "let"  channel=@KEYWORD
  #2  "[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"  channel=@DEFAULT
  #3  "[0-9]+"  channel=@DEFAULT
  #4  "+"  channel=@DEFAULT
  #5  "="  channel=@DEFAULT

tokens of "let x = 12 + y":
  keyword "let"  at 1:1
  ident "x"  at 1:5
  op "="  at 1:7
  number "12"  at 1:9
  op "+"  at 1:12
  ident "y"  at 1:14

parse succeeded, offset 14
parse of "let x = ;" failed at line 1 column 9: no PEG rule matched at offset 8

derived grammar sees 7 rules (6 inherited, 1 local)
derived parse of "0x1f" succeeded:
  number "0"
  ident "x1f"
\end{lstlisting}

Three features of the output deserve comment.

The \code{space} tokens are recorded but not printed, because the
\code{@IGNORE} channel sets the \code{ignored} flag on the match. Note that
the definition layer \emph{does} deliver the ignored match to the semantic
action: the action runs, and it is the action's own responsibility to ignore
it. This is different from the streaming matcher of
\chapref{ch:peg-combinators}, which swallows \code{@IGNORE} matches
silently, without running any action at all.

The failure message is the fixed string described above, and the line and
column point one past the offending character: the semicolon is at column
nine, and the offset is eight.

The derived grammar demonstrates the shadowing problem. The hexadecimal rule
is declared in the child, but the parent's number rule comes first in the
flattened order, so \code{"0"} is matched as a number and the hexadecimal
rule never fires; \code{"x1f"} is then matched as an identifier. The derived
grammar is not wrong, and it does not crash -- it simply cannot extend the
parent's coverage of the input, only its coverage of the gaps. To tokenise
hexadecimal literals you must either put the hexadecimal rule in the root
grammar, ahead of the number rule, or accept that the number rule will win.

\section{Negated Rules and Follow-up Rules}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-negated} is a second, smaller program that exercises
the two less common members of \code{PEGRule}. A negated rule fires where its
pattern does not match, and \code{if_succeed} runs a second rule at the same
position.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A negated rule and an \code{if_succeed} follow-up rule. The negated rule consumes exactly one character, which is why it swallows the equals sign.},label={lst:ch25-negated}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>

int
main ()
{
  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  {
    auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \t\n]+"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
    ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"let"> (
        [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "  keyword \"" << m.value << "\"\n"; });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[a-z]+"> (
        [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "  word \"" << m.value << "\"\n"; });
  }
  {
    grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+"> (
        [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "  number \"" << m.value << "\"\n"; });
  }
  {
    auto &neg = grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]"> (
        [] (dsl::PEGMatch &m)
        { std::cout << "  not a digit: \"" << m.value << "\"\n"; });
    neg.must_fail = true;
    neg.fire_on_neg = true;
  }

  // All rules exist; only now is it safe to wire pointers together.
  auto visible = grammar.rules ();
  const_cast<dsl::PEGRule *> (visible[1])->if_succeed
      = const_cast<dsl::PEGRule *> (visible[2]);

  auto result = grammar.parse ("let x = 7");
  std::cout << "parse " << (result.ok () ? "succeeded" : "failed")
            << " at offset " << result.offset << "\n";
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch25-negated}.},label={lst:ch25-negated-out}]
  keyword "let"
  word "let"
  word "x"
  not a digit: "="
  number "7"
parse succeeded at offset 9
\end{lstlisting}

The second line of the output is the \code{if_succeed} rule firing: after
the keyword rule matched \code{"let"}, the word rule was attempted at the
same position, matched \code{"let"} again, and ran its own action. The
fourth line is the negated rule: it matched where its pattern \code{"[0-9]"}
did not, and it consumed the equals sign as it did so.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}
\label{sec:ch25-limits}

The definition layer is small, and its limits are sharp.

\paragraph{Rule references dangle.}
This is the most serious defect in the subsystem and it is worth stating
without qualification. \code{add_rule} returns a reference to an element of
a \code{std::vector<PEGRule>}, and the next \code{add_rule} call may
reallocate that vector, which invalidates every reference and pointer into
it. The comment above \code{add_rule} claims otherwise, but the code does
not deliver it. A minimal reproduction is four lines:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A fragment. A dangling rule reference. The second and third \code{add_rule} calls reallocate the vector and invalidate \code{first}; the program then reads freed memory.},label={lst:ch25-dangle}]
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
auto &first = grammar.add_rule<"a"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
grammar.add_rule<"b"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
grammar.add_rule<"c"> ([] (dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
std::cout << first.pattern_text << "\n";   // undefined behaviour
\end{lstlisting}

The safe pattern is the one used throughout this chapter: configure each rule
in its own scope immediately after adding it, and take pointers to rules only
after the last \code{add_rule} call, using \code{rules()}. The shipped
\code{24-peg-matcher.cpp} does not follow this pattern; it holds
\code{PEGRule} references across further \code{add_rule} calls and then
feeds them to the streaming matcher, which is why that example prints a
stream of unknown characters instead of tokens. The example compiles, runs,
and produces no diagnostic of any kind.

\paragraph{There is no alternation, no grouping, and no recursion inside a
rule.} The pattern language of Table~\ref{tab:ch25-pattern} is the whole of
it. A rule that needs to match either of two things must be two rules, and
the ordered choice between them is decided by declaration order rather than
by the pattern.

\paragraph{There are no escape sequences.} A backslash is an ordinary
character. To match a tab, the pattern must contain a real tab, which in a
C++ string literal means a single backslash followed by \code{t}.

\paragraph{Rule patterns are compile-time constants.} The pattern is a
\code{FixedString} template argument, so it must be a string literal. There
is no API for adding a rule with a run-time pattern.

\paragraph{The layer is a lexer, not a grammar.} There is no way to express
nesting, and therefore no way to build a tree with it. See
\chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values} for what can be done instead.

\paragraph{A zero-width rule aborts the parse.} A pattern that can match
nothing -- \code{"a*"}, \code{"[a-z]?"}, or the empty pattern -- makes the
driver fail with "PEG rule matched empty input; refusing to loop" the first
time it is reached. The action of such a rule never runs, because the
zero-width check precedes the action.

\paragraph{Diagnostics are minimal.} There are exactly two failure messages,
neither of which names the rules that were tried or the character that
stopped the parse. The line and column are computed by a linear scan from
the start of the text for every match, so a long input costs a quadratic
amount of work in the diagnostics alone.

\paragraph{The \code{@IGNORE} channel is a convention, not an enforcement.}
In the definition layer, a rule on \code{@IGNORE} still runs its semantic
action and still reports its match. Only the streaming matcher treats the
channel as an instruction to suppress output.

\paragraph{Ordered choice cannot be revisited.} A rule that is tried and
fails at a position is not reconsidered at that position after some later
rule has matched, and nothing in the driver backtracks across positions. A
grammar whose rules overlap must be ordered so that the more specific rule
comes first, and there is no diagnostic that warns you when you get it
wrong.

\section{Closing}

The PEG definition layer is a fast, allocation-light tokenizer with an
ordered rule set, a channel per rule, and a callback per match. It is a
genuinely useful facility, but it is a facility for a narrower job than its
name suggests, and the narrower job is the one the shipped code implements.
Its two sharp edges are the dangling rule reference and the absence of escape
processing in the pattern compiler; both are avoidable with two lines of
discipline.

For the cases where a real grammar is needed, the toolkit provides a second
layer: the PEG combinators, which give sequence, ordered choice, repetition
and lookahead over the ordinary parser core, and a streaming matcher whose
arms are tried in the order you supply them. That layer is the subject of
\chapref{ch:peg-combinators}, and the semantic values that both layers
produce are the subject of \chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values}.
